  [1mWhy that order.[0m Notifications is a pure consumer of task changes. Its entire input surface is [3mwhat changed, on which [0m
  [3mtask, caused by whom[0m. None of those exist. If I spec notifications now, I invent a task schema to write against — and
  when the real one lands, the notification design is rework. You'd be paying for the same decisions twice.

  [1mWhy not one big greenfield spec.[0m A combined spec is big enough that the notification decisions get buried and
  under-examined, and you'd end up approving a task model chosen to serve a notification design you hadn't seen yet. Two
  specs, each small enough to actually review, is the better trade.

  [1mThe one thing I'd carry backward.[0m Design the task app so changes are [3mobservable[0m from day one: stable task IDs, and a
  record of what changed rather than only current state. That's a small seam, and it isn't speculative — you've already
  told me notifications is the destination, so it's a known requirement, not a guess. Everything else about
  notifications stays out of spec 1.

  If you'd rather not build a task app at all — say you only wanted the notification layer as a standalone thing — tell
  me and I'll re-plan.

  One answer could shrink all of this considerably, so it's my next question:
